---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Owning the Code - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="development" subChapterId="owning-the-code">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				AI generated the code, but your name is on the commit. Your name is on
				the pull request. Your name is on the project. That means you are
				responsible for understanding what it does, why it does it, and what
				happens when it breaks.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="warning" title="Non-negotiable">
				<p>
					"The AI wrote it" is not a valid excuse. Not to your team, not to your
					manager, not in a postmortem. If you shipped it, you own it.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Can You Explain It?</h3>

			<p>
				The simplest test of ownership: if someone asks you why the code works
				the way it does, can you answer? If you cannot explain a piece of code,
				you should not merge it. This applies whether you wrote it by hand,
				copied it from Stack Overflow, or had an AI generate it.
			</p>

			<p>
				When AI produces something you do not fully understand, you have options.
				Ask the AI to explain its approach. Step through the logic yourself. Write
				a test that exercises the behavior. Or rewrite it in a way that is easier to understand.
				Keep in mind the AI is fluent enough to produce explanations and code that sound right but are not (see{" "}
				<a href="/fundamentals/hallucination">Hallucination &amp; Verification</a>), so lean on tests and your own reading rather than the
				AI's confidence.
			</p>

			<p>
				Reading through what the AI produced pays off twice. You come away understanding the change, and you see what context the AI was missing:
				the convention it did not know, the constraint you forgot to mention, the file it should have looked at. That tells you what to put in your
				next prompt, so over time you direct the work better and get back less code you have to untangle.
			</p>

			<h3>Understanding Isn't Agreement</h3>

			<p>
				Being able to explain what the code does is the floor, not the ceiling. You also have to agree it is the right approach. The AI will often
				produce something that works but pulls in a dependency you would not have chosen, adds an abstraction the codebase does not need, or solves
				the problem in a way that fights your existing patterns. Understanding that code is not the same as endorsing it.
			</p>

			<p>
				When you do not agree, say so and redirect. Ask why it chose that approach, weigh the answer, and have it change course if the reasoning does
				not hold up. The goal is not to follow along with what the AI decided, it is to make the decisions yourself and use the AI to carry them out.
			</p>

			<h3>The Accountability Has Not Changed</h3>

			<p>
				Before AI tools, you were responsible for the code you wrote. With AI
				tools, you are responsible for the code you directed, reviewed, and
				approved.
			</p>

			<ul class="space-y-3 my-4">
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">You direct the work.</span>{" "}
					The quality of the output starts with the quality of your instructions.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">You review the output.</span>{" "}
					Every diff, every PR, every change should go through your judgment before
					it merges. See <a href="/development/code-review">Code Review</a> for how.
				</li>
				<li>
					<span class="font-semibold">You maintain the result.</span>{" "}
					When something breaks at 2 AM, you need to be able to{" "}
					<a href="/development/debugging">debug it</a> regardless
					of who or what originally wrote it.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<p>
				AI makes you faster at producing code. It does not make you less
				responsible for it.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					Since AI speeds up implementation, you should be making smaller, more
					frequent PRs. Size your pull requests by scope, not by how long they
					took to write. A PR that took you 20 minutes with AI should still be
					scoped the same way it would have been before.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Further Reading</h3>
			<ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600 my-4">
				<li>
					<a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/tutorials/review-ai-generated-code" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Review AI-generated code</a> — GitHub's tutorial on what to check in generated code
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://medium.com/@fahimulhaq/read-before-you-run-how-to-review-ai-code-safely-f34aa7e1904f" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read before you run: How to review AI code safely</a> — Fahim ul Haq on why "you are accountable for what you run"
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
